# Badges A (part 2): checked against official scouting.org excerpts.

@owner mb.archaeology
@complete complete
@source https://www.scouting.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Advancement-updates-2026_2.pdf | Advancement Updates 2026 (scouting.org)
@note Requirement 9 changed for 2026 and now focuses on careers. Requirements 10 and 11 each offer a choice of two options.
@desc Archaeology is the study of the human past through what people left behind. The badge covers how archaeologists find, date and protect sites, the ethics of artifacts, your own family and local history, careers, and a hands-on dig or exhibit.
1 k What archaeology is :: What archaeology is, how it differs from related fields, and how it differs from artifact collecting.
2 k The archaeological process :: The steps from locating a site to sharing what was learned.
3 k Dating the past :: Ways archaeologists date sites and artifacts, and absolute versus relative dating.
4 a Researching sites :: Researching archaeological sites in the United States and elsewhere and presenting one.
5 k Protecting sites :: Laws and agreements that protect archaeological sites, local rules, and organizations that help.
6 d Archaeological ethics :: Why sites matter, what to do if you find an artifact, and how you can help protect the past.
7 a Artifacts and family history :: A museum visit, a family heirloom, and what archaeologists might learn from a household’s trash.
8 a Local history :: A group that lived in your area long ago and what a site from that time might show.
9 a Archaeology careers :: Archaeology career options and how to prepare for one.
10 a Hands-on archaeology :: Assisting a qualified archaeologist on a project, or taking part in a simulated dig.
11 a Exhibit or experiment :: Helping prepare an exhibit, or trying experimental archaeology and reporting on it.

@owner mb.archery
@complete complete
@source https://www.scouting.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/430-93825b-Range-and-Target-Activities-Manual-FINAL.pdf | National Range and Target Activities Manual, 2026 (scouting.org)
@note Requirement 5 offers a recurve or longbow option and a compound bow option, each with its own steps and qualifying scores.
@desc Archery covers range safety, arrows and equipment, how archery is scored, and (under qualified supervision) shooting form and qualifying scores with a recurve, longbow or compound bow.
@safety Archery is done only on an approved range under a certified range supervisor, following the National Range and Target Activities Manual.
1 k! Range safety :: Projectile risks, range rules and whistle commands, safe arrow retrieval and archery laws.
2 m Arrows :: Arrow parts, types and materials, making or repairing an arrow, and arrow care.
3 k Archery gear :: Tabs, arm guards, gloves and quivers, and terms such as draw length and draw weight.
4 k Archery concepts :: Ends and rounds, field, target and 3-D archery, and scoring a target.
5 demo! Shooting :: Under qualified supervision, one bow option: bow parts and care, shooting form and a qualifying score.

@owner mb.architecture
@complete partial
@note Excerpts confirmed requirements 1, 3, 4 and 5; requirement 2 was not visible and is not outlined yet.
@desc Architecture explores how buildings shape the places we live: sustainable design and reuse, how architects work with clients and builders, measured drawings, and careers in architecture.
1 m Buildings in your community :: Touring your community’s buildings, sketching one you admire, and sustainable architecture and building reuse.
3 a Professional practice :: One option, such as reviewing models and drawings with an architect, visiting a construction site, or writing a short program for a client.
4 a A measured drawing :: Measuring a room and drawing a scaled floor plan with walls, doors, windows and built-in furniture.
5 a Architecture careers :: Architecture career options and the preparation one of them requires.

@owner mb.art
@complete complete
@desc Art explores what art is and why it matters, the elements and principles of design, creating work in several media, a design project, visiting an art venue, and careers in art.
1 d What art is :: What art is, why it matters to people and what it means to you.
2 k Elements of art :: Line, value, shape, form, space, color and texture, with examples.
3 k Principles of design :: The principles of design such as rhythm, balance, proportion and unity.
4 a Working in several media :: Rendering a subject in several different media.
5 a A design project :: One option: designing something useful, telling a story in pictures, or designing a logo.
6 a Visiting an art venue :: Visiting a museum, gallery, exhibit or artist’s workshop and discussing what you learned.
7 a Art careers :: Art career options and the preparation one of them requires.

@owner mb.artificial-intelligence
@complete complete
@source https://filestore.scouting.org/filestore/Merit_Badge_ReqandRes/Note-to-Counselor-Artificial-Intelligence-(AI)-Merit-Badge.pdf | Note to Counselor: Artificial Intelligence merit badge (scouting.org)
@note This badge has no pamphlet. A forum report suggested one sub-item in requirement 6 may be removed; check the current list.
@desc Artificial Intelligence introduces how AI and automation work, where they show up in everyday life, school and work, the ethics of bias, privacy and deepfakes, how to communicate clearly with AI tools, and a hands-on project or lesson.
1 k Key concepts :: Key terms such as artificial intelligence, machine learning, narrow and general AI.
2 k AI basics :: Everyday and workplace uses of AI, how it can support school, and milestones in its history.
3 k Automation basics :: Everyday and workplace uses of automation and milestones in its history.
4 d Ethics in AI :: Bias, privacy and AI decision-making, your own ethics guidelines, and the Turing test.
5 k Deepfakes :: What deepfakes are, their effects, and what to do if someone is targeted.
6 k Developing AI skills :: How AI learns and its limits, communicating with AI, and writing clear instructions and prompts.
7 a Practical application :: One option: an AI project, or a lesson about AI taught to a group.
8 a AI careers :: One option: researching AI and automation careers, or interviewing a professional.

@owner mb.astronomy
@complete complete
@source https://www.scouting.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/3321625-Scouts-BSA-Requirements.pdf | 2025 Scouts BSA Requirements (scouting.org)
@desc Astronomy takes you outside under the night sky: observing safely, light pollution, telescopes and binoculars, constellations, planets, the Moon and Sun, an observing activity and careers or hobbies in astronomy.
@safety Never look at the Sun without proper solar filters. Observe at night with a buddy and dress for the cold.
1 k! Safety :: Hazards of astronomy activities, first aid, clothing for night and cold, and safe solar observing.
2 k Light and air pollution :: How light and air pollution affect observing.
3 k Telescopes and binoculars :: Types of telescopes and binoculars, their parts and their care.
4 a The night sky :: Naked-eye constellations and stars, sketching a constellation, and the Milky Way.
5 a Planets :: Visible planets, when they can be seen, how they move, and observing one.
6 a The Moon :: Sketching the Moon and its phases, what keeps it in orbit, and eclipses.
7 k The Sun and stars :: What the Sun is made of, sunspots, and star colors.
8 a Observing activities :: An option such as a planetarium or observatory visit, an observing session, a star party or astrophotography.
9 a Careers or hobbies :: One option: astronomy careers, or a hobby that uses astronomy skills.
